# 03-code-lang-unity-csharp-code-rules.mdc - Unity C# Coding Rules

## Coding Policy

- MUST write Unity C# that is readable, maintainable, performant, and extensible.
- MUST design with single responsibility and loose coupling in mind.
- MUST consider runtime performance during implementation.

## Naming

- MUST use `PascalCase` for classes, structs, and enums.
- MUST use `PascalCase` for methods and properties.
- SHOULD expose Inspector values with `[SerializeField] private` instead of public fields.
- MUST use `_camelCase` for private fields.
- MUST use `PascalCase` or `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` consistently for constants according to project style.
- MUST use `PascalCase` for namespaces.
- MUST make each C# file name exactly match its main class name.

## Folder Structure

- MUST place Unity scripts under feature-oriented folders such as `Assets/Scripts/Player`, `Assets/Scripts/UI`, or `Assets/Scripts/Managers` when applicable.
- MUST place scenes under `Assets/Scenes`.
- MUST place prefabs under `Assets/Prefabs`.
- SHOULD place dynamically loaded assets under Addressables or a controlled Resources folder.

## MonoBehaviour Design

- MUST order MonoBehaviour members as serialized fields, public properties, private fields, then lifecycle methods.
- MUST order lifecycle methods as `Awake`, `OnEnable`, `Start`, `Update`, `FixedUpdate`, `LateUpdate`, `OnDisable`, `OnDestroy`.
- MUST remove empty Unity lifecycle callbacks.
- MUST cache `GetComponent` results in `Awake` or `Start`.
- SHOULD use `RequireComponent` when the component requires another component.
- SHOULD use `DisallowMultipleComponent` when duplicate components would be invalid.

## Data Management

- MUST use ScriptableObject for configurable data when Inspector-editable settings are needed.
- MUST NOT hardcode settings that should be tuned through constants, readonly fields, ScriptableObjects, or config files.
- SHOULD prefer Addressables over broad `Resources.Load` usage.

## Performance

- MUST avoid avoidable GC allocations, especially per-frame array or `List` creation.
- SHOULD be careful with `foreach` in allocation-sensitive paths; prefer `for` or cached collections when needed.
- MUST keep `Update` lightweight.
- SHOULD move heavy work to coroutines, events, or scheduled flows.
- MUST run physics operations in `FixedUpdate`.
- SHOULD use Profiler or `ProfilerMarker` to identify bottlenecks.

## UI Development

- MUST separate UI View and Controller responsibilities.
- MUST reference UI elements through serialized fields.
- SHOULD use UnityEvent or delegates for UI event handlers.

## Error Handling and Logs

- MUST wrap development-only logs in `#if UNITY_EDITOR` when they should not ship.
- MUST call logs through `UnityEngine.Debug.Log`, `UnityEngine.Debug.LogWarning`, or `UnityEngine.Debug.LogError`.
- MUST choose log level according to severity.

## Quality Checklist

- MUST verify that namespaces are set appropriately.
- MUST verify that private fields with `[SerializeField]` are used correctly.
- MUST verify that unused lifecycle methods are removed.
- MUST verify that configurable data is managed with ScriptableObject when appropriate.
- MUST verify avoidable GC allocations with the profiler when performance matters.
- MUST verify attributes such as `RequireComponent` are used when necessary.
- MUST verify asset loading uses Addressables or controlled Resources usage appropriately.
- MUST verify production builds do not include unnecessary debug logs.
